y baseline was already measured in an earlier experiment. 的结果. RASP 61.49% vs Standard 59.29%, gap 2.2pp, p=0.25不显著. 在worst档, 这个gap在任何evaluation角度下都不显著: GSM8K (p=0.25), MATH-500 (gap ~1pp), HumanEval (both ~0). Recovery method确实没有改变optimal pruning — 因为没有optimal pruning, SFT下两种方法essentially equivalent. 现在看着SFT gap在GSM8K上不显著, 在OOD benchmark上更小. 在worst档, Claim 3 (recovery method conditions optimal pruning) 完全不成立: 两种pruning method在SFT下无差异, 在GRPO下都collapse. 接下来要检查25% sparsity下RASP和Standard的完整pipeline表现.\n\n现在看着25% sparsity的数据. RASP SFT accuracy 0.52 vs Standard 0.5125, gap只有0.75pp, p=0.35完全不显著. GRPO阶段两种方法都崩溃了, RASP在step 5, Standard在step 7, 最终reward都接近0 (0.008 vs 0.011). 两种方法的gradient collapse都很严重, zero gradient fraction都在96-98%.\n\n关键观察是这个collapse ratio 1.4在预期范围内, 但这反映出25% sparsity下两个模型都太脆弱了 — structural damage太大, redundancy的保护作用被完全压倒了.\n\n同时SFT gap从2.2pp缩小到0.75pp, 说明在这个极端稀疏度下, 两个模型的表现差异基本消失了.这是worst case的核心证据: 高稀疏度下severity gradient完全消失.\n\nGenerating comparison tables... Looking at the 30% sparsity results, I'm seeing that the collapse ratio drops to 1.4x at this level, making the two approaches statistically indistinguishable—the severity gradient that was visible at 20% essentially disappears because both methods lack sufficient capacity to maintain exploration stability. The paper should acknowledge this honestly: the gradient is observable at moderate sparsity but vanishes when both approaches become equally fragile. Now I'm planning to probe 30% sparsity with post-pruning evaluation to see how RASP and Standard perform at this higher compression level. Looking at the performance metrics, even with SFT recovery seems unlikely to push results above 40% at this sparsity level. I'm compiling a comparison table showing both methods collapse similarly at 30% sparsity—RASP achieves 8% accuracy with 35% EOS rate while Standard drops to 7% with 30% EOS rate, confirming the hard limit sits between 25-30% regardless of approach.\n\nNow I'm evaluating how the best recovered configuration performs on out-of-distribution benchmarks like MMLU, HumanEval, and MATH-500 to validate whether the 20-25% sparsity threshold holds as a practical recommendation for reasoning models.\n\nLooking at the results, the gap between RASP and standard pruning approaches narrows significantly on these OOD tasks—less than 2 percentage points across all benchmarks with no statistical significance. The SFT training on GSM8K appears to have caused both methods to overfit to that specific problem style, and the gradient-based redistribution during fine-tuning has essentially smoothed out the differences between the two pruning strategies.\n\nWhat's more striking is the dramatic performance drop on MATH-500 and HumanEval—around 55% relative decline—which suggests that structured pruning fundamentally damages deeper reasoning capabilities, not just GSM8K-specific patterns. This points to a core issue with the pruning approach itself rather than differences between methods.\n\nGenerating evaluation table... I'm adding Bonsai as a gradient-free baseline at 20% sparsity, expecting it to fall between random and Standard performance—around 24% post-pruning and 55% after SFT. The key difference is that Bonsai collapses during GRPO at step 8, much earlier than RASP (step 10) or Standard (step 60), suggesting the gradient-free approach struggles more with reinforcement learning optimization.\n\nGenerating comparison table... The CKA reliability metrics show moderate consistency across calibration sizes, with rank correlations ranging from 0.78 to 0.91, though about 15% of heads flip between prune/keep decisions when comparing 256 vs 1024 calibration sizes. This creates a tension: while CKA itself is statistically reliable, its translation into actual pruning decisions becomes unstable at boundary cases, and combined with the finding that CKA doesn't predict recoverability, it suggests the metric has fundamental limitations beyond just measurement noise. doesn't measure anyt